From 3588a3ab505a0b9f716a59cb328cd2b1bab8a428 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:35:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): sort only the top k of an argsort_top_k row
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

MoE routing calls ggml_argsort_top_k, which is a full ARGSORT followed
by a view of the first k ids. The CPU kernel sorted the whole row with
std::sort, so a 512-expert router sorted 512 ids per token to read 10.
In the tg profile this ARGSORT costs about 27 us a call, against 3 us
for the SOFT_MAX on the same row.

ggml_argsort_top_k now stores k in op_params[1] of the argsort node as
a top-k hint. When the hint is at most a quarter of the row, the CPU
kernel runs std::partial_sort over the first k ids with the same
comparator. Above a quarter, it keeps std::sort, because partial_sort
is heap-based and slower when k nears the row length. The use_ref path
always sorts the whole row, so test-backend-ops -b CPU checks the hint
against a full sort.

The first k ids come out in the same descending order as before. The
rest of the row holds the remaining ids in no particular order, and
ggml.h documents this contract on ggml_argsort. The graph stays
ARGSORT + VIEW, so the CUDA, Vulkan, SYCL, Metal and OpenVINO top-k
fusions still match. Backends that ignore the hint still sort the full
row.

ggml_argsort_top_k now names the expected and the found k when k is
larger than the row.

Tests: add ARGSORT_TOP_K cases (512 x 10 for 1, 4 and 512 rows, group
selection, k at and above a quarter of the row, and k == ne0). Pass the
sort order to the power-of-two ARGSORT cases, which ran ASC twice.

The idea comes from ik_llama.cpp (MIT): its ggml_top_k sets
op_params[1], and iqk_argsort calls partial_sort when k is less than
the row length.

